preConditionHook:
// GRIMES'S STANDARD-DEVIATION SPIKE: this bar's change from the prior close
// is at least sdMultiple population standard deviations of the bar-to-bar
// close changes over the last lookback bars. Identical both sides -- the
// test is on the absolute change, so it carries no direction. A difference
// of prices throughout, so it passes the shift test.
//
// Not BarRangeAboveStd: same machinery, but that rule measures the bar's
// high-low RANGE and adds the window mean back into the band. No mean term
// here.
//
// EL's stddev() reads the chg VARIABLE's own history, and a variable holds
// its initial 0 for every bar before the strategy's first evaluated one.
// Recomputing from price instead would use warm-up bars EasyLanguage never
// saw, so the history is mirrored as a ring buffer that starts zeroed and is
// re-seeded if the length ever changes -- BarRangeAboveStd's port, followed
// exactly, INCLUDING leaving the warm-up unguarded: over mostly zeros the
// deviation is small and the rule fires easily, which is what EasyLanguage
// does and therefore what the twin must do.
// Scratch is declared in localVariables, never at hook scope, so the rule
// survives being placed twice in one strategy.
chg = close[0] - close[1];
if (chgHistory.size() != (std::size_t)(int)lookback) {
    chgHistory.assign((std::size_t)(int)lookback, 0.0);
    chgCursor = 0;
}
chgHistory[chgCursor] = chg;
chgCursor = (chgCursor + 1) % chgHistory.size();
chgMean = 0.0;
for (double v : chgHistory) {
    chgMean += v;
}
chgMean /= (int)lookback;
// Population standard deviation (divide by N): EasyLanguage's definition.
chgStd = 0.0;
for (double v : chgHistory) {
    double d = v - chgMean;
    chgStd += d * d;
}
chgStd = std::sqrt(chgStd / (int)lookback);
